      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# CHAPTER 2: Variable Declarations, Data Types and Operators\n",
        "\n",
        "---"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 1. Variable Declarations (or Lack Thereof)\n",
        "\n",
        "In most compiled languages, variables must be declared before they are used. In fact, C is even more restrictive: Variables have to be declared at the beginning of a code block and before any statements are given. Other languages, like C++ and Java, allow \"on-the-fly\" declarations i.e., those which occur in the middle of a body of code—but these name and type declarations are still required before the variables can be used. \n",
        "\n",
        "In Python, there are no explicit variable declarations. Variables are \"declared\" on first assignment. Like most languages, however, variables cannot be accessed until they are created and assigned."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Accessing an unassigned variable raises a NameError\n",
        "try:\n",
        "    print(a)\n",
        "except NameError as e:\n",
        "    print(\"NameError:\", e)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Variable assignment\n",
        "x = 4\n",
        "y = 'this is a string'\n",
        "print(\"x =\", x)\n",
        "print(\"y =\", y)\n",
        "\n",
        "# The del statement removes reference to an object\n",
        "del x\n",
        "try:\n",
        "    print(x)\n",
        "except NameError as e:\n",
        "    print(\"After del x:\", e)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "--- \n",
        "## 2. Python Statements, Indentation, and Comments\n",
        "\n",
        "### Python Statement\n",
        "Instructions that a Python interpreter can execute are called statements. For example, `x = 10` is an assignment statement. `if` statement, `for` statement, `while` statement etc. are other kinds of statements.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Multi-line Statement\n",
        "In Python, end of a statement is marked by a newline character. But we can make a statement extend over multiple lines with the line continuation character (`\\`).\n",
        "\n",
        "Line continuation is implied inside parentheses `()`, brackets `[]` and braces `{}`. We can also put multiple statements in a single line using semicolons `;`.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Python Indentation\n",
        "Most programming languages like C, C++, Java use braces `{}` to define a block of code. Python uses indentation.\n",
        "A code block (body of a function, loop etc.) starts with indentation and ends with the first unindented line. The amount of indentation is up to you, but it must be consistent throughout that block. Generally four whitespaces are used for indentation and are preferred over tabs."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Explicit line continuation\n",
        "a = 10 + 12 + 35 + \\\n",
        "    40 + 50 + 60 + \\\n",
        "    70 + 80 + 19\n",
        "print(\"Explicit continuation a =\", a)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Implied line continuation\n",
        "a = (10 + 12 + 35 +\n",
        "     40 + 50 + 60 +\n",
        "     70 + 80 + 19)\n",
        "print(\"Implicit continuation a =\", a)\n",
        "\n",
        "colors = ['silver',\n",
        "          'yellow',\n",
        "          'red']\n",
        "print(\"Colors:\", colors)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Multiple statements in a single line\n",
        "a = 10; b = 20; c = 30\n",
        "print(\"a, b, c =\", a, b, c)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Indentation example\n",
        "for i in range(1, 10):\n",
        "    print(i)\n",
        "    if i == 6:\n",
        "        break\n",
        "\n",
        "# Single line conditional block\n",
        "if True: print('Hello Learner'); x = 5 + 7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "--- \n",
        "## 3. Comments & Multiple Assignments\n",
        "\n",
        "### Python Comments\n",
        "Comments are very important while writing a program. It describes what's going on inside a program so that a person looking at the source code does not have a hard time figuring it out.\n",
        "* **Single-line comment:** Use the hash (`#`) symbol.\n",
        "* **Multi-line comment:** Use hash (`#`) on each line or triple quotes (`'''` or `\"\"\"`).\n",
        "\n",
        "### Multiple Assignments\n",
        "Python allows assigning values to multiple variables simultaneously or assigning the same value to multiple variables."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Single-line comments example\n",
        "# This is a comment\n",
        "print('Hello Learner\\'s')\n",
        "\n",
        "\"\"\"\n",
        "This is also a\n",
        "perfect example of\n",
        "multi-line comments\n",
        "\"\"\"\n",
        "\n",
        "# Multiple assignments\n",
        "a, b, c = 5, 3.2, \"Education\"\n",
        "print(\"a, b, c:\", a, b, c)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Assign same value to multiple variables\n",
        "a = b = c = 100\n",
        "print(\"a, b, c:\", a, b, c)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Assigning a tuple\n",
        "a = 10, 20, 30\n",
        "print(\"Tuple a:\", a)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "--- \n",
        "## 4. Python Variables and Data Types\n",
        "\n",
        "A variable is a location in memory used to store some data (value). Data types represent the category of values.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Python Numbers\n",
        "Python supports four numerical types:\n",
        "1. **int**: Signed integers.\n",
        "2. **long**: Long integers (legacy/large representation).\n",
        "3. **float**: Floating point real values.\n",
        "4. **complex**: Complex numbers ($x + yj$).\n",
        "\n",
        "| Number Type | Examples |\n",
        "| :--- | :--- |\n",
        "| **Int** | `0101`, `84`, `-237`, `0x80`, `0x92` |\n",
        "| **Long** | `29979062458L`, `-84140l`, `0xDECADE` |\n",
        "| **Float** | `3.14159`, `4.2E-10`, `-90.`, `6.022e23`, `-1.609E-19` |\n",
        "| **Complex** | `6.23+1.5j`, `-1.23-875J`, `0+1j`, `9.80665-8.31441J`, `-.0224+0j` |\n",
        "\n",
        "> **Note:** Integers can be of any length, limited only by available memory. Floating point numbers are accurate up to 15 decimal places."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Large Integers\n",
        "a = 1234567890123456789\n",
        "print(\"a =\", a)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Float truncation / Precision\n",
        "b = 0.1234567890123456789\n",
        "print(\"b =\", b)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Complex numbers\n",
        "c = 1 + 2j\n",
        "print(\"c =\", c)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Type verification\n",
        "a = 5\n",
        "print(a, \"is of type\", type(a))\n",
        "\n",
        "a = 5.0\n",
        "print(a, \"is of type\", type(a))\n",
        "\n",
        "a = 1 + 2j\n",
        "print(a, \"is complex number?\", isinstance(1 + 2j, complex))"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "--- \n",
        "## 5. Python Strings & Slicing\n",
        "\n",
        "Strings in Python are identified as a contiguous set of characters represented in quotation marks (single, double, or triple quotes). Subsets of strings can be taken using the slice operator (`[]` and `[:]`)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "str1 = 'Python'\n",
        "str2 = 'is an easy language!'\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"str1[0]:\", str1[0])\n",
        "print(\"str1[2:5]:\", str1[2:5])\n",
        "print(\"str2[:2]:\", str2[:2])\n",
        "print(\"str2[3:]:\", str2[3:])\n",
        "print(\"str2[-1]:\", str2[-1])\n",
        "print(\"Concatenation:\", str1 + str2)\n",
        "print(\"Concatenation with space:\", str1 + ' ' + str2)\n",
        "print(\"Repetition:\", str1 * 2)\n",
        "print(\"Dot repetition:\", '.' * 20)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "--- \n",
        "## 6. Python Collections (List, Tuple, Set, Dictionary)\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1. Python List\n",
        "An ordered sequence of items. All items in a list do not need to be of the same type. Lists are **mutable** (elements can be altered).\n",
        "\n",
        "### 2. Python Tuple\n",
        "An ordered sequence of items same as list. The only difference is that tuples are **immutable** (cannot be modified after creation).\n",
        "\n",
        "### 3. Python Set\n",
        "An **unordered collection of unique items**. Defined by values separated by commas inside braces `{}`. Indexing has no meaning in sets.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 4. Python Dictionary\n",
        "An **unordered collection of key-value pairs**. It is optimized for retrieving data when the key is known."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# List operations\n",
        "a = [5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 35, 40]\n",
        "print(\"a[2] =\", a[2])\n",
        "print(\"a[0:3] =\", a[0:3])\n",
        "print(\"a[5:] =\", a[5:])\n",
        "a[2] = 4\n",
        "print(\"Mutated list a:\", a)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Tuple operations & Immutability test\n",
        "t = (5, 'program', 1 + 3j)\n",
        "print(\"t[0:3] =\", t[0:3])\n",
        "try:\n",
        "    t[0] = 10\n",
        "except TypeError as e:\n",
        "    print(\"Tuple Error:\", e)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Set operations & Uniqueness\n",
        "a = {5, 2, 3, 1, 4}\n",
        "print(\"Set a =\", a)\n",
        "a_dup = {1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3}\n",
        "print(\"Duplicates eliminated:\", a_dup)\n",
        "try:\n",
        "    print(a_dup[1])\n",
        "except TypeError as e:\n",
        "    print(\"Set Indexing Error:\", e)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Dictionary operations\n",
        "d = {1: 'ONE', 'key': 2}\n",
        "print(\"type(d):\", type(d))\n",
        "print(\"d[1] =\", d[1])\n",
        "print(\"d['key'] =\", d['key'])\n",
        "try:\n",
        "    print(d[2])\n",
        "except KeyError as e:\n",
        "    print(\"KeyError:\", e)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "--- \n",
        "## 7. Conversion Between Data Types\n",
        "\n",
        "We can convert between different data types using explicit conversion functions like `int()`, `float()`, `str()`, `set()`, `tuple()`, `list()`, and `dict()`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Scalar Type Conversion\n",
        "print(\"float(5):\", float(5))\n",
        "print(\"int(100.6):\", int(100.6))\n",
        "print(\"int(-1.6):\", int(-1.6))\n",
        "print(\"float('2.5'):\", float('2.5'))\n",
        "print(\"str(25):\", str(25))\n",
        "\n",
        "try:\n",
        "    int('TN10')\n",
        "except ValueError as e:\n",
        "    print(\"ValueError:\", e)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Sequence Conversion\n",
        "print(\"set([1,2,3]):\", set([1, 2, 3]))\n",
        "print(\"tuple([5,6,7]):\", tuple([5, 6, 7]))\n",
        "print(\"list('Hello Chennai'):\", list('Hello Chennai'))\n",
        "print(\"dict([[1,2],[3,4]]):\", dict([[1, 2], [3, 4]]))\n",
        "print(\"dict([('101','HDCA'),('102','ADJP')]):\", dict([('101', 'HDCA'), ('102', 'ADJP')]))"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "--- \n",
        "## 8. Python Input, Output and Formatting\n",
        "\n",
        "### The `print()` function\n",
        "Syntax:\n",
        "```python\n",
        "print(*objects, sep=' ', end='\\n', file=sys.stdout, flush=False)\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "### Formatting Options\n",
        "* **`str.format()` method:** Uses `{}` as placeholders.\n",
        "* **`%` Operator:** Old `sprintf()` style formatting (e.g., `%d` for integer, `%f` for float)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Output with sep and end\n",
        "print(1, 2, 3, 4)\n",
        "print(1, 2, 3, 4, sep='*')\n",
        "print(1, 3, 5, 7, sep='#', end='&\\n')\n",
        "\n",
        "# Output formatting using format()\n",
        "x = 5; y = 10\n",
        "print('The value of x is {} and y is {}'.format(x, y))\n",
        "print('I would like eat {0} and {1}'.format('Pizza', 'Burger'))\n",
        "print('I had visited {1} and {0}'.format('Delhi', 'Mumbai'))\n",
        "print('Hello {name}, {greeting}'.format(greeting='Good morning', name='Ramesh'))\n",
        "\n",
        "# Output formatting using % operator\n",
        "x = 12.3456789\n",
        "print('The value of x is %3.2f' % x)\n",
        "print('The value of x is %3.4f' % x)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "--- \n",
        "## 9. Python Input & Import\n",
        "\n",
        "### Python `input()` Function\n",
        "Syntax: `Variable = input([prompt])`. `input()` always reads user input as a **string**.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Python Import\n",
        "When a program grows bigger, Python definitions and statements can be broken into modules using `.py` extension. We use the `import` keyword to use modules."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Evaluating numeric expressions safely or typecasting input\n",
        "val_str = \"10\"\n",
        "print(\"Type of input:\", type(val_str))\n",
        "print(\"Converted to int:\", int(val_str))\n",
        "\n",
        "# eval() evaluates string expression\n",
        "expr = '2 + 3'\n",
        "print(\"eval('2+3'):\", eval(expr))\n",
        "\n",
        "# Importing modules\n",
        "import math\n",
        "print(\"math.pi =\", math.pi)\n",
        "\n",
        "from math import pi\n",
        "print(\"pi directly =\", pi)\n",
        "\n",
        "import sys\n",
        "print(\"sys.path length =\", len(sys.path))"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "--- \n",
        "## 10. Python Operators Documentation & Tables\n",
        "\n",
        "Operators are special symbols in Python that carry out arithmetic or logical computation.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1. Arithmetic Operators\n",
        "\n",
        "| Operator | Meaning | Example |\n",
        "| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n",
        "| `+` | Add two operands or unary plus | `x + y + 2` |\n",
        "| `-` | Subtract right operand from left or unary minus | `x - y - 2` |\n",
        "| `*` | Multiply two operands | `x * y` |\n",
        "| `/` | Divide left operand by right one (always results into float) | `x / y` |\n",
        "| `%` | Modulus - remainder of the division of left operand by right | `x % y` |\n",
        "| `//` | Floor division - division that results into whole number adjusted to left | `x // y` |\n",
        "| `**` | Exponent - left operand raised to the power of right | `x ** y` |\n",
        "\n",
        "### 2. Comparison Operators\n",
        "\n",
        "| Operator | Meaning | Example |\n",
        "| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n",
        "| `>` | Greater than - True if left operand is greater than the right | `x > y` |\n",
        "| `<` | Less than - True if left operand is less than the right | `x < y` |\n",
        "| `==` | Equal to - True if both operands are equal | `x == y` |\n",
        "| `!=` | Not equal to - True if operands are not equal | `x != y` |\n",
        "| `>=` | Greater than or equal to | `x >= y` |\n",
        "| `<=` | Less than or equal to | `x <= y` |\n",
        "\n",
        "### 3. Logical Operators\n",
        "\n",
        "| Operator | Meaning | Example |\n",
        "| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n",
        "| `and` | True if both the operands are true | `x and y` |\n",
        "| `or` | True if either of the operands is true | `x or y` |\n",
        "| `not` | True if operand is false (complements the operand) | `not x` |\n",
        "\n",
        "### 4. Bitwise Operators\n",
        "\n",
        "| Operator | Meaning | Example |\n",
        "| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n",
        "| `&` | Bitwise AND | `x & y` |\n",
        "| <code>\|</code> | Bitwise OR | <code>x \| y</code> |\n",
        "| `~` | Bitwise NOT | `~x` |\n",
        "| `^` | Bitwise XOR | `x ^ y` |\n",
        "| `>>` | Bitwise right shift | `x >> 2` |\n",
        "| `<<` | Bitwise left shift | `x << 2` |\n",
        "\n",
        "### 5. Assignment Operators\n",
        "\n",
        "| Operator | Example | Equivalent to |\n",
        "| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n",
        "| `=` | `x = 5` | `x = 5` |\n",
        "| `+=` | `x += 5` | `x = x + 5` |\n",
        "| `-=` | `x -= 5` | `x = x - 5` |\n",
        "| `*=` | `x *= 5` | `x = x * 5` |\n",
        "| `/=` | `x /= 5` | `x = x / 5` |\n",
        "| `%=` | `x %= 5` | `x = x % 5` |\n",
        "| `//=` | `x //= 5` | `x = x // 5` |\n",
        "| `**=` | `x **= 5` | `x = x ** 5` |\n",
        "| `&=` | `x &= 5` | `x = x & 5` |\n",
        "| `|=` | `x |= 5` | `x = x | 5` |\n",
        "| `^=` | `x ^= 5` | `x = x ^ 5` |\n",
        "| `>>=` | `x >>= 5` | `x = x >> 5` |\n",
        "| `<<=` | `x <<= 5` | `x = x << 5` |\n",
        "\n",
        "> **Note:** Python does not support unary increment/decrement operators such as `n++` or `--n`.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 6. Special Operators\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Identity Operators (`is`, `is not`)\n",
        "\n",
        "| Operator | Meaning | Example |\n",
        "| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n",
        "| `is` | True if the operands are identical (refer to the same object) | `x is True` |\n",
        "| `is not` | True if the operands are not identical | `x is not True` |\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Membership Operators (`in`, `not in`)\n",
        "\n",
        "| Operator | Meaning | Example |\n",
        "| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n",
        "| `in` | True if value/variable is found in the sequence | `5 in x` |\n",
        "| `not in` | True if value/variable is not found in the sequence | `5 not in x` |"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Arithmetic Operators Example\n",
        "x = 15; y = 4\n",
        "print('x + y =', x + y)\n",
        "print('x - y =', x - y)\n",
        "print('x * y =', x * y)\n",
        "print('x / y =', x / y)\n",
        "print('x // y =', x // y)\n",
        "print('x ** y =', x ** y)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Comparison Operators Example\n",
        "x = 10; y = 12\n",
        "print('x > y is', x > y)\n",
        "print('x < y is', x < y)\n",
        "print('x == y is', x == y)\n",
        "print('x != y is', x != y)\n",
        "print('x >= y is', x >= y)\n",
        "print('x <= y is', x <= y)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Logical Operators Example\n",
        "x = True; y = False\n",
        "print('x and y is', x and y)\n",
        "print('x or y is', x or y)\n",
        "print('not x is', not x)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Bitwise Operators Example (x = 10 -> 1010, y = 4 -> 0100)\n",
        "x = 10; y = 4\n",
        "print('x & y =', x & y)\n",
        "print('x | y =', x | y)\n",
        "print('~x =', ~x)\n",
        "print('x ^ y =', x ^ y)\n",
        "print('x >> 2 =', x >> 2)\n",
        "print('x << 2 =', x << 2)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Identity Operators Example\n",
        "x1 = 5; y1 = 5\n",
        "x2 = 'Hello'; y2 = 'Hello'\n",
        "x3 = [1, 2, 3]; y3 = [1, 2, 3]\n",
        "print('x1 is not y1:', x1 is not y1)\n",
        "print('x2 is y2:', x2 is y2)\n",
        "print('x3 is y3:', x3 is y3)  # False because lists are distinct mutable objects\n",
        "\n",
        "# Membership Operators Example\n",
        "x = 'Hello world'\n",
        "y = {1: 'a', 2: 'b'}\n",
        "print(\"'H' in x:\", 'H' in x)\n",
        "print(\"'hello' not in x:\", 'hello' not in x)\n",
        "print(\"1 in y:\", 1 in y)\n",
        "print(\"'a' in y:\", 'a' in y)  # False, checks keys not values"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "--- \n",
        "## 11. Solved Problem\n",
        "\n",
        "**Problem Statement:** Write a program to input $N$ metres and convert it to equivalent number of feet, inches, and centimetres.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Formulae:\n",
        "* $1 \\text{ Foot} = 12 \\text{ Inches} = 30.48 \\text{ cm}$\n",
        "* $1 \\text{ Inch} = 2.54 \\text{ cm}$\n",
        "* $1 \\text{ Metre} = 39.3701 \\text{ Inches}$"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Solved Problem Program\n",
        "N = float(input(\"Enter Number of metre(s): \") if 'input_override' in globals() else 5)\n",
        "\n",
        "nofinchs = N * 39.3701\n",
        "nofeets = nofinchs / 12\n",
        "nofcms = nofinchs * 2.54\n",
        "\n",
        "print('No. of metres =', N)\n",
        "print('No. of Feets =', nofeets)\n",
        "print('No. of Inches =', nofinchs)\n",
        "print('No. of cms =', nofcms)"
      ]
